From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:12:55 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 23/24] perf: VU1 programs in round-toward-zero mode (vurecomp
 --rz), and a bench for them

With --rz, compiled programs leave arithmetic results to the host, which the runtime switches to round-toward-zero and flush-to-zero around each compiled program (FPCR on ARM64, MXCSR on x86-64) and restores afterwards. A finite result then never overflows to infinity and a tiny one becomes a signed zero, which is what vfix did after every operation, and it is the VU's own rounding. Multiply-add products are kept from being fused into one FMA. Operands loaded from memory still go through vfix.

UN5 battle on a Switch at 1785/768/1600 MHz: ~37 to ~43 fps; the hottest program 25.7 to 22.2 us per call. PS2_VU_CHECK (both sides in the same mode) still differs from the interpreter only in unread flags. Results differ from round-to-nearest in the last bit, e.g. no pixel of a battle replay frame moved by more than 24 of 255.

PS2_VU_BENCH=<key> times a program from the state of its first run and compares it with stand-ins registered through register_native; PS2_VU_BENCH_RUNS sets the count.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
